  only language with the world. Ships read it the way others read scripture, and she had never once
  let it fail.

  The winter her husband drowned, she did not weep until spring. She simply kept climbing.

  Now the station was automated, a stubby white tower with a sensor box bolted to its side, and the
  mainland office had sent a letter with the phrase "your services are no longer required." Edith
  read it twice at the kitchen table, folded it into quarters, and put it in the drawer with the
  tide tables.

  On her last night, a fog rolled in thick as wool. She climbed the steps anyway, out of habit and
  something less easy to name. At the top, the lamp hummed its steady tune, capable without her.

  She stood at the rail and listened. Somewhere out in the murk, an engine throbbed, slow and
  uncertain. A fishing boat, maybe, its radar dead, its crew squinting at nothing.

  Edith went inside. She did not switch off the automatic system. Instead she hauled the old
  hand-crank foghorn from its storage locker, the one nobody had sounded since 1987, and she cranked
  it. The sound rolled out low and enormous, a bellow across the water.

  She cranked for four hours. Her forearms burned. Once she thought she heard an answering horn,
  faint and to the east, and she cranked harder.

  At dawn the fog lifted in slow white curtains. A trawler sat a mile off the rocks, its deck crew
  raising a hand toward the tower. Edith raised hers back.

  Later, a letter arrived from the company: a formal commendation, and a pension she had not
  expected. She read it, folded it, and put it with the others.

  Then she walked down the one hundred and twelve steps for the last time, her hand trailing the
  cold rail. She did not look back at the light, because she knew it would keep burning without her,
  and that, she decided, was what a good keeper hoped for.

